<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">French Ionian Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1797 CE – 1814 CE · capital Corfu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ionian Islands under French rule after the fall of Venice, twice annexed by France between 1797 and 1814 and finally held only at Corfu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> destruction of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice</a> in 1797 brought French troops to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in October 1797 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/venetian-ionian-islands">Venetian Ionian Islands</a> and their mainland outposts, which were organised as three French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corcyre-department">Corcyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaque-department">Ithaque</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mer-egee">Mer-Egee</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The French burned the Golden Book of the Venetian nobility, planted liberty trees and introduced revolutionary institutions, but their requisitions and anticlericalism alienated many islanders. A Russian-Ottoman expedition under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-ushakov">Ushakov</a> took the islands in 1799, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/septinsular-republic">Septinsular Republic</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaty of Tilsit</a> in 1807 returned them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> blockaded and captured most of the islands in 1809-1810, but the French governor General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-xavier-donzelot">Donzelot</a> held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a> until it was surrendered in 1814 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> abdication. The islands then became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-of-the-ionian-islands">United States of the Ionian Islands</a> under British protection.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-ionian-islands.jpg" alt="French Ionian Islands" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1797 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corfu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Napoleon, Francois-Xavier Donzelot</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Italian, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French departments of Greece, Departements francais de Grece</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=french-ionian-islands" aria-label="French Ionian Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5172 759 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Ionian Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5492 1015L5492 1015M5492 1015L5054 728M5492 1015L5492 1015"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5492 1015h0M5054 728h0M5492 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5492 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5492 1015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Corfu. <a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=french-ionian-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
